<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

  <!--
    Why this file exists.

    Without an application manifest, Windows runs the process as *system* DPI
    aware: it is told the display's scaling once, at startup, and everything
    drawn afterwards is scaled by the compositor as a bitmap. The result is a
    blurry UI on any display that is not at 100%, and a window that is wrong the
    moment it is dragged onto a second monitor with a different scale factor -
    Windows will not re-render it at the new DPI because the process never said
    it could handle that.

    PerMonitorV2 is the mode that fixes both: the process is notified when the
    DPI of the window changes, and WPF re-renders at the new scale rather than
    stretching. The two dpiAware entries below it are the fallbacks for Windows
    versions that do not know PerMonitorV2 - Windows 8.1 and Windows 8 - so the
    app is per-monitor aware on those and system aware on anything older.

    Nothing else is declared here on purpose:
      * No requestedExecutionLevel. The default is asInvoker, which is what this
        app needs - it must never prompt for elevation, and saying so explicitly
        invites the question of why it was mentioned.
      * No supportedOS list. The app targets Windows 10 and later through its
        target framework, and getting that list wrong changes version-checking
        behaviour in ways that are hard to notice.
  -->

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2, PerMonitor</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>

</assembly>
